The File System Access API lets a web app open a folder the user picks with` window.showDirectoryPicker()` , list its contents through a` FileSystemDirectoryHandle` , read files with` getFile()` , and write them back with` createWritable()` . Nothing reaches the disk until you call` close()` .


If you build a notes editor, a log viewer or an image organiser, upload forms are a poor fit. Users already keep these files in folders, and they expect your tool to edit those folders directly, not hand them copies to download. This article builds one small Markdown notes app that does that. It opens a folder, lists the` .md` files, edits one, saves it back, and reopens the same folder on the next visit. The app works on real folders the user can see. If you need storage the browser keeps hidden instead, see[running SQLite in the browser with OPFS](https://blog.openreplay.com/sqlite-browser-opfs/) .


## Key Takeaways


- ` showDirectoryPicker()` needs a secure context and a user gesture such as a button click. Without the gesture, it throws` SecurityError` .
- An` AbortError` from` showDirectoryPicker()` means no folder is available. Return to idle and don’t show an error.
- Writes made through a` FileSystemWritableFileStream` land on disk only when` close()` resolves.
- You can store a` FileSystemDirectoryHandle` in IndexedDB. On a later visit, check it with` queryPermission()` and ask again with` requestPermission()` from a click.
- ` <input type="file" webkitdirectory>` works in all major browsers, but it only reads. To save on that path, you have to download a Blob.


## What Does the File System Access API Make Possible?


With the File System Access API, a web app can open a folder the user chooses, read every file in it, and, once the user grants write permission, save changes to those same files. In the notes app, the user picks` ~/Documents/notes` . The app lists` ideas.md` and` todo.md` , the user edits one in a textarea, and Save overwrites the original file. No copy lands in Downloads. The picker comes from the[WICG File System Access spec](https://wicg.github.io/file-system-access/) . Handles, reading and writing come from the[WHATWG File System Standard](https://fs.spec.whatwg.org/) .


## Open a Folder From a Button Click


To open a local folder, call` window.showDirectoryPicker()` inside a click handler, after checking that the API exists.[MDN documents three options](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/showDirectoryPicker) :


- ` id` makes the picker remember the last location it used.
- ` mode` is` 'read'` by default, or` 'readwrite'` .
- ` startIn` is a well-known folder such as` 'documents'` .


The method resolves to a` FileSystemDirectoryHandle` .


```text
const   supportsFSA   =   '  showDirectoryPicker  '   in   window   &&   window  .  self   ===   window  .  top  ;


let   notesDir  ;      // FileSystemDirectoryHandle
let   currentFile  ;   // FileSystemFileHandle


openBtn  .  addEventListener  (  '  click  '  ,   async   ()   =>   {
try   {
notesDir   =   await   window  .  showDirectoryPicker  ({
id  :   '  notes  '  ,   mode  :   '  readwrite  '  ,   startIn  :   '  documents  '  ,
});
}   catch   (  err  ) {
if (  err  .  name   ===   '  AbortError  '  )   return  ;   // no folder; stay idle
throw   err  ;
}
await   saveHandle  (  notesDir  );
await   listNotes  (  notesDir  );
});
```


` showDirectoryPicker()` only works in a secure context and must be called from a user gesture. If you call it on page load, it throws a` SecurityError` , and the same-origin policy produces the same error in a cross-origin iframe. The` window.self === window.top` check rejects every iframe, which is stricter than the policy requires. That check is the detection approach used in the[web.dev open-a-directory pattern](https://web.dev/articles/files/open-a-directory) .


` AbortError` covers more than a cancelled dialog. The browser also raises it when it refuses a folder it considers sensitive, or when permission for the requested mode isn’t granted. So the handler above returns quietly rather than showing a “you cancelled” message.


## List Entries and Open Subfolders


A` FileSystemDirectoryHandle` is an async iterable:` for await (const entry of dir.values())` gives you each child, and each child is already a handle. Check[entry.kind](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemHandle/kind) for` 'file'` or` 'directory'` . Iteration order isn’t guaranteed, so sort the results before you display them.


```text
async   function   listNotes  (  dir  ) {
const   notes   =   [];
for await (  const   entry   of   dir  .  values  ()) {
if (  entry  .  kind   ===   '  file  '   &&   entry  .  name  .  endsWith  (  '  .md  '  ))   notes  .  push  (  entry  );
}
notes  .  sort  ((  a  ,   b  )   =>   a  .  name  .  localeCompare  (  b  .  name  ));
list  .  replaceChildren  (  ...  notes  .  map  ((  h  )   =>   {
const   li   =   document  .  createElement  (  '  li  '  );
li  .  textContent   =   h  .  name  ;
li  .  onclick   =   ()   =>   openNote  (  h  );
return   li  ;
}));
}
```


To recurse, pass each directory entry straight back into the walker. To jump to a subfolder you already know the name of, use` getDirectoryHandle(name)` :


```text
async   function  *   walk  (  dir  ,   path   =   ''  ) {
for await (  const   entry   of   dir  .  values  ()) {
if (  entry  .  kind   ===   '  directory  '  )   yield  *   walk  (  entry  ,   `${  path  }${  entry  .  name  }  /  `  );
else   yield   {   handle  :   entry  ,   path  :   path   +   entry  .  name   };
}
}


const   drafts   =   await   notesDir  .  getDirectoryHandle  (  '  drafts  '  );
```


## Read a File With getFile()


Calling[getFile() on a FileSystemFileHandle](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemFileHandle/getFile) gives you a` File` , which is a` Blob` . Read text with` .text()` and binary data with` .arrayBuffer()` . If you know the file name,` dir.getFileHandle('todo.md')` skips the iteration step.


```text
async   function   openNote  (  handle  ) {
const   file   =   await   handle  .  getFile  ();
editor  .  value   =   await   file  .  text  ();
currentFile   =   handle  ;
}
```


The` File` is a snapshot. If another program changes the file afterwards, call` getFile()` again to see the new contents.


## Write a File: Permission, createWritable, Close


To write a local file with the File System Access API, you need` readwrite` permission. Call[createWritable()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemFileHandle/createWritable) to get a stream, pass it strings, Blobs or buffers, then close it. Requesting` mode: 'readwrite'` in the picker gets the user’s consent up front, so the first save doesn’t need to ask.


```text
saveBtn  .  addEventListener  (  '  click  '  ,   async   ()   =>   {
if (  !  currentFile  )   return  ;   // no note open yet
saveBtn  .  disabled   =   true  ;
try   {
const   writable   =   await   currentFile  .  createWritable  ();
await   writable  .  write  (  editor  .  value  );
await   writable  .  close  ();   // contents reach disk here
}   finally   {
saveBtn  .  disabled   =   false  ;
}
});
```


Changes written to a[FileSystemWritableFileStream](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemWritableFileStream) reach the file on disk only when you call` close()` . Always await it. The handler keeps the Save button disabled until it resolves.


To create a new note, call` notesDir.getFileHandle('new-note.md', { create: true })` and write to it the same way.


## Remember the Folder Between Visits


You can store a` FileSystemDirectoryHandle` in IndexedDB and read it back on a later visit. Holding the handle doesn’t guarantee access, though, so check permission and ask again before you read or write. The[MDN File System API overview](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/File_System_API) notes that handles can be serialized to IndexedDB.


```text
const   db   =   ()   =>   new   Promise  ((  res  ,   rej  )   =>   {
const   r   =   indexedDB  .  open  (  '  notes-app  '  ,   1  );
r  .  onupgradeneeded   =   ()   =>   r  .  result  .  createObjectStore  (  '  handles  '  );
r  .  onsuccess   =   ()   =>   res  (  r  .  result  );
r  .  onerror   =   ()   =>   rej  (  r  .  error  );
});


async   function   saveHandle  (  h  ) {
const   tx   =   (  await   db  ()).  transaction  (  '  handles  '  ,   '  readwrite  '  );
tx  .  objectStore  (  '  handles  '  ).  put  (  h  ,   '  notesDir  '  );
return   new   Promise  ((  res  ,   rej  )   =>   {   tx  .  oncomplete   =   res  ;   tx  .  onerror   =   ()   =>   rej  (  tx  .  error  ); });
}


async   function   loadHandle  () {
const   req   =   (  await   db  ()).  transaction  (  '  handles  '  ).  objectStore  (  '  handles  '  ).  get  (  '  notesDir  '  );
return   new   Promise  ((  res  ,   rej  )   =>   {   req  .  onsuccess   =   ()   =>   res  (  req  .  result  );   req  .  onerror   =   ()   =>   rej  (  req  .  error  ); });
}
```


Check access with[queryPermission()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemHandle/queryPermission) . If it doesn’t return` 'granted'` , ask with[requestPermission()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemHandle/requestPermission) , which resolves to` 'granted'` ,` 'denied'` or` 'prompt'` .` requestPermission()` must be called from a user gesture, so trigger it from a button click, never on load.


```text
async   function   ensureReadWrite  (  handle  ) {
const   opts   =   {   mode  :   '  readwrite  '   };
if ((  await   handle  .  queryPermission  (  opts  ))   ===   '  granted  '  )   return   true  ;
return   (  await   handle  .  requestPermission  (  opts  ))   ===   '  granted  '  ;
}


const   saved   =   await   loadHandle  ();
if (  saved  ) {
reopenBtn  .  hidden   =   false  ;
reopenBtn  .  onclick   =   async   ()   =>   {
if (  await   ensureReadWrite  (  saved  )) {   notesDir   =   saved  ;   listNotes  (  saved  ); }
else {   reopenBtn  .  hidden   =   true  ;   openBtn  .  hidden   =   false  ; }
};
}
```


If permission is denied, the code shows the regular “Open folder” button again, and the user’s next click opens the picker. The[Chrome 122 persistent permissions update](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/persistent-permissions-for-the-file-system-access-api) lets users choose “Allow on every visit”, and installed apps keep the permission automatically. In other cases access may last only for the session, so always call` queryPermission()` before you use a stored handle.


## Browser Support and the webkitdirectory Fallback


` showDirectoryPicker()` works in Chromium-based browsers: Chrome and Edge on desktop, and Chrome for Android from version 132. Firefox and Safari don’t support it, and MDN marks it Experimental with Limited availability. In those browsers,[<input type="file" webkitdirectory>](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLInputElement/webkitdirectory) still reads a whole folder, and MDN lists it in every major browser (Chrome 7, Edge 13, Firefox 50, Safari 11.1). What you get is a read-only list of` File` objects, each with a` webkitRelativePath` , and no way to write back to the folder.


```text
function   pickFolderFallback  () {
return   new   Promise  ((  resolve  )   =>   {
const   input   =   Object  .  assign  (  document  .  createElement  (  '  input  '  ), {   type  :   '  file  '  ,   webkitdirectory  :   true   });
input  .  addEventListener  (  '  change  '  , ()   =>   resolve  ([  ...  input  .  files  ].  filter  ((  f  )   =>   f  .  name  .  endsWith  (  '  .md  '  ))));
input  .  addEventListener  (  '  cancel  '  , ()   =>   resolve  (  null  ));
input  .  click  ();
});
}


function   downloadNote  (  name  ,   text  ) {
const   url   =   URL  .  createObjectURL  (  new   Blob  ([  text  ], {   type  :   '  text/markdown  '   }));
Object  .  assign  (  document  .  createElement  (  '  a  '  ), {   href  :   url  ,   download  :   name   }).  click  ();
setTimeout  (()   =>   URL  .  revokeObjectURL  (  url  ),   0  );
}
```


Listen for the input’s[cancel event](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLInputElement/cancel_event) , or the Promise never settles.` cancel` also fires in place of` change` when the user selects the same folder again. On this path, Save means a download, as covered in[creating a downloadable file in the browser](https://blog.openreplay.com/create-downloadable-file-browser/) . To let users drop files in as well, add the patterns from[a custom file upload component with drag and drop](https://blog.openreplay.com/custom-file-upload-react/) .


