## Conclusion


Supporting real local folders comes down to four calls and one rule. Pick the folder from a click, iterate the handle, read with` getFile()` , and write through a stream that saves only on` close()` . Then store the handle in IndexedDB, and put the permission check behind a button so the user can reopen their folder on the next visit. Add the` webkitdirectory` fallback with download-to-save, test both paths over HTTPS, and your notes app will work in every major browser and write back to the folder wherever the API is supported.


## FAQs


Does createWritable() overwrite the whole file or append to it?


It overwrites by default. createWritable() starts from an empty temporary file, so the data you write replaces the entire file when close() resolves. To keep the current contents, pass { keepExistingData: true }, which copies the existing file into the temporary file first. To append, call seek() with the file's current size before writing. The original file does not change until the stream is closed.


How do I delete a file or subfolder inside a folder opened with showDirectoryPicker()?


Call removeEntry(name) on the parent FileSystemDirectoryHandle, for example notesDir.removeEntry('old-note.md'). The handle needs readwrite permission, or the call throws NotAllowedError. Removing a folder that still has entries throws InvalidModificationError unless you pass { recursive: true }. If no entry with that name exists, it throws NotFoundError. Ask the user to confirm before deleting anything.


Can users drag a folder onto the page instead of using the picker?


Yes, in Chromium-based browsers. In the drop event handler, call getAsFileSystemHandle() on each DataTransferItem. A dropped folder resolves to a FileSystemDirectoryHandle, the same type that showDirectoryPicker() returns, so the same listing, reading and IndexedDB code works on it. Call it on every item before any await, because the handle is only available in the same tick as the drop event. Firefox and Safari do not support it.


